    desc: "Coast Salish weavers — women spinning, men sometimes carving the tools — used two-hand spindles weighted by large wooden whorls up to thirty centimeters across, carved with thunderbirds, wolf faces and stylized spirit heads, sometimes painted red and black. Wool from mountain goats and from the now-extinct Salish wool dog, plus cattail and fireweed fibers, fed a robe-weaving industry whose blankets were wealth, marriage goods and potlatch currency long before the fur trade made them famous. Whorls doubled as heirlooms and ritual objects, and broken pieces often lived second lives cached in middens and graves. Museums from the Burke to the Museum of Anthropology in Vancouver hold the classic corpus, and Musqueam artist Susan Point's public works turned whorl geometry into Vancouver's contemporary civic iconography. The look: rotating carved faces, wool-white robes, cedar-shadow interiors.",
