    sum: "Steam-bent wooden visors — painted open-crown chagudax that shaded the kayak hunter's eyes and ranked his deeds at the waterline.",
    desc: "Unangan men hunted sea otters from skin kayaks wearing chagudax: hats bent from a single driftwood plank, steamed and shaped into long-billed visors or open-crown crowns that shaded vision toward the sea glare while keeping spray off. Paint in red ochre, charcoal and later trade pigments banded the surface; sea-lion whiskers, ivory ornaments, bird beaks and glass beads signaled a hunter's rank and harvests, with whisker counts read like regalia. Bentwood technology — softening wood in hot water, clamping to dry — also built boxes and the famous multi-hatch baidarkas. Russian-era collectors carried visors into European and American museums, and the Anchorage Museum and Smithsonian Arctic Studies Center hold benchmark examples. Contemporary Unangan artists have revived chagudax carving as a flagship heritage art. The look: elongated lacquered bills, whisker fringe, sea-light.",
