    sum: "Quetzal, cotinga and hummingbird plumes inlaid like jeweler's mosaic — shields, fans and the great penacho of Mexica state splendor.",
    desc: "The amanteca, Tenochtitlan's featherworker guild, turned tropical plumes into the most valued regalia of the Mexica world, documented in the Florentine Codex. Two techniques dominated: feathers sewn and tied in layered cords for flexible garments, and fine mosaic feathers glued face-down onto a paper-and-cotton backing to build smooth iridescent fields. Tribute rolled quetzal tails from cloud forests, cotingas, hummingbirds and orioles into the capital, where they became chimalli shields, fans, priestly ornaments and warrior devices — the famous penacho attributed to Moctezuma II now anchors Vienna's Kunsthistorisches Museum. After the conquest the guild pivoted to Christian feather 'paintings' for churches and export. Museum fragments from Mexico City to Vienna preserve the palette: emerald, turquoise-blue, crimson and gold. The look: jewel-toned sheen shifting with every breath of air.",
