    sum: "Bug-powder red and jade green frescoes — the Paradise of Tlaloc and the Great Goddess coating apartment-compound walls in the ancient megacity.",
    desc: "Teotihuacan, a city of perhaps 100,000, coated its apartment compounds in fine lime plaster painted with mineral pigments in a controlled palette of cinnabar reds, jade greens and creamy whites. The Tepantitla compound's 'Paradise of Tlaloc' shows a mountain streaming water, goggle-eyed rain spirits, butterfly-souled dead and ballplaying figures; the Great Goddess of Tetitla spills water and jade from her hands under a feathered headdress. Painting is flat, profile-driven and notation-like — figures built from repeated glyphic modules rather than optical perspective. Porticoes, courtyards and temple substructures carried programmatic friezes of processions, jaguars and plumed serpents. INAH's long excavation and conservation campaigns keep the style central to Mesoamerican art history, and its motifs traveled as far as Maya courts. The look: chalky luminous color in disciplined bands.",
