    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "carved royal monuments", e: "historical", o: "Classic Maya courts — Yaxchilan, Piedras Negras, Quiriguá, Copán", g: "Usumacinta and Motagua valleys (Guatemala, Mexico)",
    p: "c. 300-900 CE", era: "Classic Maya apogee",
    sum: "Limestone stelae and lintels — k'uhul ajaw kings in ritual dress, pinned to Long Count dates by dense hieroglyphic texts.",
    desc: "Classic Maya kings commissioned freestanding limestone stelae and doorway lintels as instruments of time-keeping and legitimacy: a ruler in profile, jaguar kilt and quetzal-feather headdress, dominates a scene of ritual — bloodletting, capture, ballplay — while Initial Series dates bind the image to exact calendar reckonings. Yaxchilan's Lintel 24 shows Lady Xoc drawing a thorned rope through her tongue before King Shield Jaguar; Quiriguá's Stela E rises over eight meters, the tallest Maya monolith. Carvers worked shallow, crisp relief with dense costume texturing and glyph blocks as compositional mass. Many monuments were 'k'altun' — ritually bound or wrapped at period endings — and buried or toppled at collapse around 900 CE. The British Museum, Penn Museum and Guatemala's national museum hold the signature corpus. The look: deep limestone shadows reading as courtly drama.",
