    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "backstrap-and-tension wool weaving", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Rarámuri (Tarahumara) weavers", g: "Sierra Madre Occidental, Chihuahua (Copper Canyon)",
    p: "colonial wool era to present", era: "living tradition",
    sum: "Handspun wool sashes and blankets in aniline pinks and madder reds — the woven signature of Copper Canyon runners.",
    desc: "Rarámuri women weave sheep's wool — a colonial introduction absorbed completely into Sierra life — on simple staked tension looms set up in patios and cave houses, producing sashes, blankets, headbands and skirt panels famous for saturated aniline fuchsias and reds banded with cream and black. Warp-faced striping, handspun irregularity and long twisted fringes mark the work, which dresses everyday life and festival alike: men's cloth headbands and the women's layered skirts are closed with woven belts. Weaving remains a household economy threaded through the canyon's tourist trails and mission markets, with FONART and Chihuahua cooperative fairs carrying award weavings outward. Unlike neighboring Pueblo and Navajo traditions, Rarámuri loomwork stays small-format and portable, tuned to a life of running the barrancas. The look: hot pink and chili red stripes glowing against canyon stone.",
    col: [{ h: "#c2447e", n: "aniline fuchsia" }, { h: "#a63a2b", n: "madder red" }, { h: "#efe6d0", n: "cream wool" }, { h: "#3c6b4f", n: "pine green" }, { h: "#d9a441", n: "ochre gold" }],
