    p: "19th c codification to present", era: "living tradition",
    sum: "Carved goat-bearded masks, cocoon rattles and deer-hoof anklets — the pascola and deer dancers who anchor Yaqui fiestas.",
    desc: "At the pahko — the fiesta that carries weddings, Lent observances and patron-saint days — the pascola (pahko'ola, 'old man of the fiesta') dances in a carved wooden mask with a long goat-hair beard, scarves and a belt strung with tenabaris, butterfly-cocoon rattles that keep the rhythm under harp-and-violin music. Beside him, the deer dancer (maso) wears antlered headgear and deer-hoof ankle rattles, enacting the deer-singer repertoire that remembers the desert animal world. Mask carving, with incised cross-and-flower designs and mirrors, is a recognized Sonoran art form; Arizona's Pascua Yaqui community and Sonoran villages maintain the tradition through active dance lines. Spanish mission theater, Mayo sibling forms and desert cosmology all feed the ceremony. The look: white goat-hair beards swinging over carved brown faces, cocoon shimmer, dust and harp strings.",
