    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "monolithic cult carving", e: "historical", o: "Chavín cult", g: "Conchucos valley, Áncash (north-central Peruvian highlands)",
    p: "c. 1200-500 BCE", era: "Early Horizon cult center",
    sum: "Snarling Lanzón god, staff-bearing Raimondi Stele and caiman Tello Obelisk — jaguar-fused monoliths in the Andes' first great cult style.",
    desc: "At 3,180 meters in the Conchucos valley, the Chavín temple fused two building stones — white granite and gray limestone — into courts, sunken plazas and labyrinthine galleries housing the Lanzón: a four-and-a-half-meter granite blade carved as a smiling-fanged deity pinned between heaven and earth. Walls wore nail-headed tenon heads of transforming jaguar-men; freestanding stones — the Raimondi Stele's Staff God with its clawed, serpentine hair, and the Tello Obelisk's double caiman — pushed a visual grammar of figure-within-figure transformation. Pilgrims traveling trade routes spread the Chavín style across the Andes, making it the first pan-Peruvian religious art, and its staff-god lineage runs straight to Tiwanaku's Gateway of the Sun. UNESCO inscribed the site in 1985; Lima's national museum keeps the Raimondi Stele. The look: mirror-polished fangs and iterated serpent locks.",
    col: [{ h: "#8d8a82", n: "granite gray" }, { h: "#e6dfd0", n: "limestone cream" }, { h: "#46403a", n: "wet-stone dark" }, { h: "#5c6b4a", n: "valley moss" }, { h: "#b08d3f", n: "hallucinogen gold" }],
