    p: "c. 600-1000 CE", era: "Middle Horizon empire",
    sum: "Thread-count-tight camelid tapestry tunics — abstracted staff gods and stepped frets worn like imperial uniforms across Middle Horizon Peru.",
    desc: "Wari weavers compressed entire world-views into tunics of astonishing density — tapestry weave in camelid wool reaching well over a hundred threads per inch, each shirt a single uninterrupted cloth with armhole slits and no waste. Designs abstract the Tiwanaku-related Staff God into modular rows of eyes, wings and stepped frets, so figures read as pattern and pattern as power; the controlled color fields — deep reds, ochres, teals, plums — came from the Andean dye shelf of cochineal, chillca and indigoid blues. Tunics, four-pointed hats and woven caps seem to have ranked a state workforce, prefiguring Inca uniformity by four centuries. Lima's national museum and Dumbarton Oaks hold benchmark tunics and hat types, and every surviving fragment is studied thread by thread. The look: saturated micro-geometry humming across camelid nap.",
