    desc: "Tiwanaku's ceremonial core assembled sandstone slabs and hard andesite blocks with joints so fine a blade resists, arranged into the Akapana stepped pyramid, the Kalasasaya platform and the Semi-Subterranean Temple studded with nailed-in stone heads of many faces. The Gateway of the Sun — a single andesite portal — carries the classic frieze: a frontal Staff God with ray-crowned tears, flanked by ranks of running profile attendants, an image some read as a calendar and all read as power. Giant stelae like the Bennett Monolith, sunken courts and precise drainage made the city the Middle Horizon's pilgrimage magnet, feeding styles into Wari and memory into the later Inca, who trekked here to claim descent. UNESCO inscribed Tiwanaku in 2000, and the La Paz museum keeps the Bennett Monolith. The look: cold gray megastone against thin blue sky.",
