    p: "c. 800-1537 CE", era: "pre-conquest highland chiefdoms",
    sum: "Hammered, gilded tunjos, snakes and the Muisca raft — lake offerings that fed the El Dorado legend.",
    desc: "Muisca smiths worked gold-copper tumbaga by hammering sheets into flat 'palette' figures — tunjos — plus snakes, frogs and bird-form pendants, then depletion-gilded surfaces so the objects gleamed like solid gold. The signature genre is the votive raft: the famous Muisca raft, recovered from a cave at Pasca in 1969, shows a chieftain attended by oarsmen and standard-bearers, a direct image of the Guatavita lake ritual in which a dusted ruler offered gold to the waters — the ceremony that launched El Dorado. Offerings were sunk into lakes and caves rather than hoarded, so survival is chance and precious. Spanish conquistadors drained and dredged Guatavita, and Bogotá's Museo del Oro now guards tens of thousands of pieces including the raft. The look: hammered gold-light figures flattened like dawn reflections on water.",
