    sum: "Serene lost-wax poporo lime flasks — the pear-and-helmet Poporo Quimbaya that became the Museo del Oro's emblem.",
    desc: "Quimbaya goldsmiths of the Middle Cauca valley cast some of the ancient Americas' most assured metalwork by lost wax: poporos — lime flasks used with coca chewing — drawn as smooth ovoids with schematic serene faces, plus helmets, nose ornaments, pectorals and lime-dipper sticks worked in mirror planes and fine filigree. The Poporo Quimbaya, dated to the classic era, entered Bogotá's Museo del Oro in 1939 as its founding masterpiece and now serves as the museum's logo. The 1891 'Quimbaya Treasure' hoard was sold to Spain and sits in Madrid's Museo de América, a standing case study in colonial-era collecting and repatriation debate. Smooth-surfaced casting, negative-space handles and balanced silhouettes distinguish Quimbaya work from hammered Muisca flatness. The look: warm gold geometry with a still human face.",
