    sum: "Fanged volcanic-tuff gods and jaguar-mouth altars standing guard over slab tombs across the San Agustín mesetas.",
    desc: "Across a high green valley of the Upper Magdalena, Agustinian carvers raised hundreds of volcanic-tuff statues — around five hundred survive — on funerary mounds and slab-chamber tombs, the largest sculptural ensemble of pre-Columbian Colombia. Figures range from stern ancestors in turban-like headdresses to fanged, jaguar-mouthed beings whose mouths swallow altars or smaller figures; warriors clutch clubs and trophies, and some stones stack two bodies in one column. Quarried blocks were carved with harder stone tools, painted traces suggest, and aligned with tomb architecture rather than free display. The Parque Arqueológico de San Agustín plus Alto de los Ídolos and Alto de las Piedras preserve the main groups, UNESCO-listed since 1995; meaning is debated — shamans, ancestors, guardian beings. The look: moss-eaten gray tuff faces looming over Andean mist.",
