    sum: "Zemi icons of stone, wood and cotton — three-pointed cemis, cohoba spatulas and duho seats of Taíno sacred power.",
    desc: "Zemi (cemí) named both the Taíno's powerful beings and the objects embodying them: three-pointed trigonolito stones, wooden figures carved in dense macorí wood, cotton-bodied ancestor bundles, elbow-stand duho stools for chiefs, and spit-tube vomit spatulas for cleansing before cohoba snuff ceremonies that carried shamans to spirit audiences. Faces stress flat planes, wide orbital rings and toothy, contained mouths; three-pointer cosmology tied mountains, yucca and ancestors into portable forms cached in houses and caves. Columbus-era Spanish collecting stripped the islands early, so survivals are scarce and contested, but parks of trigonolitos, the Caguana ball courts in Puerto Rico, and museum corpora — El Museo del Barrio, the British Museum's duhos — keep the tradition legible to Taíno-descended communities. The look: pocket-boulder deities with ringed eyes and patient grins.",
