    sum: "Macaw and parrot plumes fanned into solar disks — the red-and-yellow burst of Bororo ceremony in central Brazil.",
    desc: "Bororo ceremonial life turns on moieties and clans whose color and species names organize bodies into living geometry, and featherwork is its most photographed expression: headdresses (cocars), armbands, belts and fans built from macaw, parrot and toucan plumes mounted on palm-fiber cordage with resin bindings, fanning into disks of scarlet, yellow, green and blue. Specialist makers assemble each piece for named dances and funerary obligations, when regalia accompanies the soul's passage; the red-and-yellow round cocar reads like a sunrise against painted bodies. Karl von den Steinen's 1880s expeditions carried Bororo plumes into European museums — the quai Branly and Brazil's Museu do Índio hold classic suites — while villages such as Meruri and Tadarimana keep production and meaning local. The look: saturated feather solar disks over dark painted skin.",
