    sum: "Hain initiation huts where painted 'spirits' — white geometry over blackened skin — taught Selk'nam boys the Moon's hidden law.",
    desc: "The Hain was the Selk'nam male initiation: a long hut ceremony in which masked and body-painted performers personified spirits of the Moon myth to instruct klóketen initiates in cosmology, lineage and adult discipline. Painters covered bodies in white and grey clay geometric fields — diagonals, dots, star-lines — accented in red ochre, turning each performer into a named supernatural with its own remembered pattern. Martín Gusinde's 1919-1924 photographs and Anne Chapman's later recordings froze the repertoire just as sheep estancias, gold rushes and epidemics ended the last ceremonies in the early twentieth century. Chilean and Argentine museums plus the pre-Columbian museum in Santiago hold the documentation, and Selk'nam descendants have staged paint revivals onstage. The look: chalk-white geometry breathing on firelit skin.",
