    desc: "Oruro's Carnaval — UNESCO-inscribed in 2001 — pivots on the diablada: fraternidades of dancers in winged, velvet-caped devil costumes with towering masks of repoussé tin, brass and now fiberglass, horns crowned with serpents, toads and dragons over mirror-studded faces. The dance enacts the battle between archangels and the Seven Deadly Sins for the salvation of souls, marching Saturday's procession from the mines' worldview to the Virgen del Socavón, patron of miners and the 'Tío' pact underground. Brotherhoods like the Conjunto Folclórico Diablada Urus drill for months; mask workshops in Oruro pass repoussé technique through families, and the China Supay — the devil's seductive wife — adds the tradition's most flirtatious figure. Morenada, caporales and other dances orbit the core. The look: mirrored horns flashing against altiplano dust and brass bands.",
