    sum: "Pre-dawn Boxing Day and New Year rush-outs — goat-skin drums, cowbells and whistles under crepe-paper costumes that shred by sunrise.",
    desc: "Junkanoo turns Nassau's Bay Street into a river of rhythm twice a year: from the small hours of Boxing Day and New Year's morning, troupes like the Saxons, Valley Boys and One Family rush in segmented 'pieces' of costume built from crepe paper fringed and glued over wire-and-cardboard frames, themed to outrage — giant shrimp, flamingos, historical pageants. The sound is handmade pulse: goombay drums in goat skin, clanging cowbells, brass whistles whipping dancers and crowd into call-and-answer. Origins trace through the John Canoe masquerade documented across the colonial Caribbean, with emancipation days and West African memory braided in; Jamaica's Jonkonnu is a sibling. Judging at Rawson Square decides the year's bragging rights, and the National Art Gallery of The Bahamas and Junkanoo World collections document the craft. The look: fringed color avalanches in flashlight strobe.",
