    desc: "Vodou ceremonies open with flagbearers — hounsi kanzo in white — carrying two satin drapo saluted at the poteau mitan before the lwa arrive, and the flags themselves are masterworks: thousands of glass sequins and beads stitched onto satin to translate vèvè cornmeal diagrams, lwa portraits like Ezili and Damballa, and Catholic-saint hybrids into glittering emblems. Flag-making runs through temple ateliers and Port-au-Prince workshops whose production since mid-century has moved between ritual necessity and the international art market, with Fowler Museum and American Museum of Natural History collections documenting both registers. Design follows the spiritual grammar — colors and symbols belong to specific lwa — while technique rewards density: layered sequin scales, bead-lined contours, flying cord fringes. The look: satin-dark ground alive with sequin light in ritual colors.",
